ch collaborate to ensure the safety and quality of gluten-free products across Europe.\n\n## FACE's central role in gluten-free certification\n\nFACE, or Federación de Asociaciones de Celiacos de España, is a pivotal organization in the community, responsible for certifying gluten-free products in Spain. It plays a crucial role in ensuring that these products meet strict regulations, thereby safeguarding the health of individuals with celiac disease. FACE's certification process is vital for consumers who rely on gluten-free products, as it guarantees that items bearing its mark contain gluten levels below 10 parts per million (ppm). This certification is essential for maintaining public trust and safety in gluten-free food options [Data: Entities (7952); Relationships (4116, 15708, 16889, 16891, 16888, +more)].\n\n## Collaboration with the Gluten-Free Certification Organization\n\nThe Gluten-Free Certification Organization works closely with FACE to certify gluten-free products, ensuring they are safe for individuals with celiac disease. This collaboration is significant as it combines resources and expertise to enhance the reliability of gluten-free labeling. The organization adheres to stringent criteria for certification, which is crucial in a landscape where gluten labeling can vary widely. This partnership strengthens the community's ability to provide safe food options for those affected by gluten intolerance [Data: Entities (10487); Relationships (17932, 16893)].\n\n## AOECS's regulatory influence across Europe\n\nThe Association of European Coeliacs (AOECS) plays a vital role in regulating gluten-free certification across Europe. It oversees the certification process and ensures that member associations, including FACE, adhere to established standards for gluten-free labeling. AOECS's influence is critical in maintaining consistency and safety in gluten-free products available to consumers, thereby supporting individuals with celiac disease throughout Europe [Data: Entities (7959); Relationships (15709, 16909)].\n\n## Importance of the European License System\n\nThe European License System (ELS) is a certification process that allows industries to use the gluten-free symbol, regulated by AOECS. This system is essential for ensuring that products labeled as gluten-free meet the necessary safety standards. The ELS certification is a key component in the community's efforts to provide safe food options, as it helps consumers identify products that are genuinely gluten-free, thus reducing the risk of health issues for those with celiac disease [Data: Entities (7965); Relationships (16891, 16912)].\n\n## Global implications of gluten-free certification\n\nThe lack of federal regulation for gluten labeling in countries like the United States highlights the importance of organizations like the Gluten-Free Certification Organization. This situation underscores the need for reliable certification bodies to ensure the safety of gluten-free products, especially for individuals with celiac disease. The community's efforts in Europe can serve as a model for other regions, emphasizing the necessity of stringent gluten-free standards to protect public health [Data: Entities (10487); Relationships (4471)].", "# Health Challenges and Research in Europe\n\nThe community encompasses various health-related entities in Europe, focusing on issues such as diabetes, obesity, cancer detection, and cardiovascular disease. The interconnectedness of these entities highlights the continent's diverse health landscape and the ongoing research efforts aimed at addressing these challenges.\n\n## Diabetes as a major health issue in Europe\n\nDiabetes mellitus is a significant health concern affecting millions across Europe, with varying prevalence rates among different countries. This widespread issue necessitates comprehensive public health strategies and interventions to manage and mitigate its impact. The relationship between diabetes and the broad