er health outcomes, particularly for those with gastrointestinal sensitivities. The relationship between processed foods and fructans indicates a need for awareness regarding food choices among individuals managing SII, as these foods can significantly impact their digestive health. [Data: Entities (9779); Relationships (17858)]\n\n## Visceral sensitivity's connection to SII\n\nVisceral sensitivity refers to the heightened response of individuals to gastrointestinal stimuli, which can contribute to the symptoms experienced by those with SII. This sensitivity may explain why certain dietary components, such as fructans, lead to more pronounced symptoms in affected individuals. Understanding the role of visceral sensitivity is crucial for developing effective management strategies for SII, as it highlights the need for personalized dietary approaches. [Data: Entities (9776); Relationships (17960)]", "# Irritable Bowel Syndrome and Food Intolerance Community\n\nThis community focuses on the interrelated conditions of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and food intolerances, highlighting the significance of dietary management strategies such as the Low FODMAP diet. The entities within this community are interconnected through their shared symptoms and dietary implications, indicating a complex relationship that affects individuals' health and well-being.\n\n## Irritable Bowel Syndrome as a central condition\n\nIrritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a prevalent gastrointestinal disorder that serves as the focal point of this community. It is characterized by symptoms such as abdominal pain, bloating, and altered bowel habits, which can severely impact daily life. The complexity of IBS is compounded by its association with food intolerances, making it essential for individuals to identify and manage their dietary triggers. The relationship between IBS and food intolerances is critical, as many individuals with IBS report exacerbated symptoms when consuming certain foods. [Data: En